{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000541","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates to the target trait (uterine cancer) are present, including PGS000541 and PGS000542 (both ‘Malignant neoplasm of uterus’ with trait_efo ‘uterine cancer’) and PGS001795 (‘Uterine cancer’, trait_efo ‘uterine carcinoma’). Endometrial carcinoma models are also plausibly aligned as the clinically dominant subtype per the provided domain knowledge, but they are not exact-label uterine cancer endpoints.

PGS000541 is selected as best-supported because it is an exact concept match (trait_efo uterine cancer; phenotyping_reported ‘Malignant neoplasm of uterus’), comes from a cancer-PRS repository with explicit evaluation in EUR, and has coherent PRS-associated effect size evidence (per-1SD OR 1.311 [1.209–1.422] and top-1% vs rest OR 2.12 [1.17–3.84]). Between the two near-identical exact-label siblings (PGS000541 vs PGS000542), PGS000541 has slightly higher AUROC (0.576 vs 0.572) and slightly higher pseudo-R² (0.0133 vs 0.0126), providing a small but consistent edge.

Main limitation: overall evidence strength remains limited/heterogeneous—these exact-label uterine cancer models show modest discrimination, while the genome-wide framework model (PGS001795) is exact-label but comes from a global biobank framework context and reports very small PRS-only R² (0.001948) with only a full-model AUROC (0.66) that is not directly comparable as a PRS metric per domain guidance. The presence of multiple plausible direct matches without a clearly dominant performance profile keeps confidence at Moderate and the outcome at DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS005262",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 7 candidates are direct matches to the target concept (nodular goiter), with two aligning to nontoxic multinodular goiter (PGS001814, PGS002022) and three aligning to benign nodular goiter (PGS005261–PGS005263). Two additional models (PGS005273, PGS005274) are case-control contrasts of thyroid carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter, which are not clean standalone nodular-goiter risk scores despite containing the target phenotype.\n\nAmong the clean nodular-goiter endpoints, PGS005262 is best supported by the visible evidence because it is a disease-focused multi-cohort score (global multi-ancestry thyroid disease study context) with a genome-wide shrinkage method (PRSCS) and the highest reported discrimination among the benign nodular goiter models (full_model AUROC 0.5933 vs 0.5854 and 0.5876). It also has a very large variant count (1,085,173), which is structurally favorable within this sibling set.\n\nMain limitation: covariates are listed as \"Unknown\" for these disease-focused models, and the available performance is only full-model AUROC (domain guidance cautions against inferring PRS quality from full-model AUROC alone). The UKB portability models report only partial-r (~0.024–0.028) and come from a framework/portability context, but they at least disclose covariates; this leaves some unresolved uncertainty, so the recommendation is not high-confidence."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004563",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 3 candidates are direct matches to urolithiasis via aligned endpoints (ICD N20 / calculus of kidney and ureter; EFO nephrolithiasis/ureterolithiasis). PGS001250 is from a known pan-trait UKB sparse framework paper ('across 813 traits') and uses a time-to-event endpoint, which domain guidance flags as weaker when disease-focused alternatives exist. Between the two disease-endpoint siblings from the same 2024 Commun Biol publication (PGS004493 LDpred2 vs PGS004563 RFDiseasemetaPRS), both have the same phenotype (N20), same ancestry context (DEV/EVAL EUR), and missing comparable performance metrics (AUC/R2 all null) with covariates listed as Unknown; the only differentiator visible is effect size, where PGS004563 reports a higher OR (1.283 vs 1.227). However, because key comparable performance and covariate details are missing for both 2024 models, the selection is not strongly supported, lowering confidence and making the overall direct-match choice sub-optimal rather than high-quality."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000327",
  "confidence": "High",
  "rationale": "Both candidates are direct matches on trait_reported/trait_efo (autism spectrum disorder) and are EUR-trained/evaluated. However, PGS000327 has substantially stronger endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported is explicitly 'Autism spectrum disorder' vs PGS002790 'Psychiatric behavior (withdrawn)'), and it comes from an ASD-focused publication context ('Identification of common genetic risk variants for autism spectrum disorder.'). It also shows stronger reported performance (R²=0.0245 and OR=1.33 [1.30–1.36]) compared with the weaker partial R²=0.00546 for PGS002790 (with no effect sizes reported). Main limitation: both models lack PRS-only AUC/R² and are EUR-only, so generalizability and pure-PRS discrimination cannot be fully assessed from the provided metadata."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000327",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Both candidates are direct matches on trait_reported/trait_efo, but PGS000327 has clearly better endpoint fidelity: phenotyping_reported is explicitly “Autism spectrum disorder,” whereas PGS002790’s phenotyping_reported is “Psychiatric behavior (withdrawn),” indicating an endpoint mismatch despite the ASD label fields. PGS000327 also comes from an ASD-focused Nat Genet study and reports coherent effect/performance evidence in European evaluation (OR 1.33; full_model R²=0.0245), whereas PGS002790 reports a weaker partial R² (~0.0055) and no effect sizes. Main limitation: neither model provides PRS-only metrics (e.g., PGS-only R²/AUC), so confidence is constrained to the visible full-model/partial-R² metadata and endpoint alignment."
}
